00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  just  five years ago it was hard to imagine  socialists winning elections in america  it's the united states the global heart  of capitalism  for a long time socialism was a dirty  word  republicans called everyone from harry  truman to bill clinton  a socialist in a few short years  everything has changed  a lifelong democratic socialist almost  became the democratic presidential  nominee  twice polls show socialism is soaring in  popularity  and a huge number of young socialist  people of color  are getting elected to office socialists  like jamal bowman in new york and  corey bush in missouri are defeating  incumbent congress congresspeople  black and brown working-class socialists  are winning seats in state legislatures  and city councils  and the biggest name in the democratic  party is a latina socialist  from the bronx if you're excited about  creating a society that works for  everyone  you might be inspired by socialist big  victories in 2018 and 2020  and you might be wondering if you too  can become a voice for your community  that's what i'm here to talk about i  teach math at a middle school in crown  heights brooklyn  i'm an activist and a member of the  democratic socialist of america  and in 2020 i became the first black gay  man to be elected a state senator in new  york  it wasn't easy in the democratic primary  i ran against a state assembly person  who was endorsed by the incumbent  i was opposed by the local political  machine but in the end we won with 58  of the vote it took a lot of hard work  but we won  and i want to help you do the same thing  so  here are some things to consider if you  want to run for office as a socialist  first know why you're running for office  most people who run for office either  democrat or republican  run because they have a big ego or they  want a career in politics and it's easy  to feel like  they're the ones who belong in office  but socialists have a different approach  socialism stresses community and  solidarity  you can run for office to help people in  your community people you know  people you encounter every day at work  or in the neighborhood  in my class i had one girl who was tired  all the time  and when i checked in it was because she  was up late because she had to sleep at  her grandma's house  it was obvious she didn't have a secure  place to sleep every night  how could she learn when she didn't have  her basic needs met another student of  mine was struggling and  then one day just stopped showing up a  few days later i got an email from his  mom saying they had to move to long  island because  they were evicted i ran for office  because of experiences like these  i ran to help people like my students  and their families  i knew what our system was doing to  people and how it impacted the ability  of my students to give their all in the  classroom  that leads me to my second point we need  more  working class socialist in office but  you don't have to start by running for  office  i started out by campaigning for  same-sex marriage in new york  i organized rallies for black lives  matter a canvas for alexandria  ocasio-cortez  i worked on access to affordable housing  with the democratic socialist of america  i wasn't new to these struggles by the  time i ran for office  i'd been fighting for years and was part  of a broader movement for justice  movements are made up of activists and  organizers and the best campaigns are  powered by people  you can do an incredible amount of good  by working as a tenant organizer  or contributing to unionization  campaigns even though the media likes to  focus on the big electoral victories  the real progress comes from thousands  of small victories  fought for by people you might never  hear about  over a thousand people volunteered on my  campaign for state senate  and they are the reason we won if you do  want to run for office  consider where you are most needed there  are a ton of really important positions  in local or state politics  that don't get as much attention as  senate or congressional races  but are incredibly influential  judgeships  city or town council seats state  legislatures school boards  positions at the county level so much  power is held in offices  with elections that go uncontested you'd  be surprised how far you can go  third focus on the material issues that  shape the lives of your constituents  socialism is about material change  helping people in real  tangible ways with programs and policies  that ensure everyone has access to free  health care  decent housing and a good education  don't run  on technocratic tweaks be ambitious  campaign on systems that ensure people  have what they need to survive  and thrive talk about the issues you've  experienced in your community  is rent too expensive do nurses and  doctors  not have enough funding do local schools  not have enough money  socialism is also about power who has it  and who doesn't that's why socialist  candidates need to run on structural  solutions  finding ways to bring power back to  ordinary people  talk with people in your community every  day about the problems they face  and the future you want to build  together your single biggest advantage  is your community and fourth  if you lose don't be afraid to try again  in 2017 i ran for city council and came  up short  it was tough but a few years later i  tried again running for state senate and  won  so don't be discouraged if you don't win  in 2018  corey bush ran for congress in st louis  and lost to a 20-year incumbent  two years later she came back and won  socialists are winning elections because  we're focused on creating a system  that works for everyone and because we  recognize that the system today  is designed to benefit only a small  wealthy minority  socialists are winning because we're  building power by working together  day after day and people across the  country are on board  no matter where you live there are  people all around you  eager for change and seeking practical  tangible solutions to the injustices  they face  so my last piece of advice is this  remember that you are not alone  if you're thinking about running for  office as a socialist look for other  organizing groups in your area  and online together we can create a  better society  one that works for all of us i'm jabari  brisport new york state senator  for the grivel institute  [Music]  do  [Music]  you

